  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [rchaud](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=rchaud) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26664687) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659792) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26660478)\[–\]<br>Probably the people that bought the previous Android iteration of this, the FxTec Pro 1 from 2019. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [crocsarecool](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=crocsarecool) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26660478) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26664687) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26661743)\[–\]<br>I’ve actually heard people with certain handicaps find they can type better with the physical keyboard. I’m not sure what those handicaps are - I read it a long time ago. I think one thing may have been poor eyesight and being able to feel the keys helped aid them. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [Erlich\_Bachman](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=Erlich_Bachman) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26661743) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26660478) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26667559)\[–\]<br>\> born and raised touching the screen<br>What does this have to do with the market? People don't want physical keyboards because they are "used to" them. They want because the experience of typing is different. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [ansible](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ansible) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26661879) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26661743) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26667559)\[–\]<br>I would have bought this style of phone 8 or 9 years ago. I had the original G1 (actually the ADP1) and Samsung Sidekick 4G, and _loved_ the keyboards on those phones. With some practice, I could get close to 30 wpm on the keyboards.<br>Since those phones were current though, I've needed to switch to "normal" smartphones without physical keyboards. At first I really disliked them, but with practice, and especially with better swipe-capable keyboards, I got used to it.<br>Now, I don't know that I'd worry about going back. I'd rather have a phone with long-term software support. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [knolax](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=knolax) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26667559) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26661743) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659919)\[–\]<br>IMO autocorrect only works well if all of the following are true:<br>1\. You only speak one language, and never find yourself in a situation where you encounter transliterated foreign words or names.<br>2\. You never use any obscure or technical words. No jargon or slang.<br>3\. You also never find yourself in a situation where you have to come up with new terms and abbreviations.<br>4\. You never use the keyboard for any notation other than prose. No code.<br>5\. You agree 100% with the orthographic choices of whoever made your autocorrect.<br>Personal experience tells me that as a result anyone who has autocorrect on tends to be a boring conversation partner. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [iujjkfjdkkdkf](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=iujjkfjdkkdkf) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659919) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26667559) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659880)\[–\]<br>I often have a shell open on my phone, but far less often than I would if there were good options for typing. I'm very excited by the prospect. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [mulletbum](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=mulletbum) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659880) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659919) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659988)\[–\]<br>I honestly found keyboards like this cumbersome because I can only use two fingers. I thought that was there reason we got away from them in the first place? Then haptic feedback made it so touching the screen feels better, but the original iPhone showed you didn't need a keyboard. I am so confused on who would buy this device. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [HideousKojima](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=HideousKojima) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659987) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659880) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26660148)\[–\]<br>>I thought that was there reason we got away from them in the first place?<br>Yes, and that reason was "Apple did it and everyone else imitated them." There were other reasons too, having a keyboard on the phone itself like a Blackberry took up valuable screen real estate, and slide-outs made the phone ticker (when all manufacturers were crazy about making them thinner and thinner) and adds a mechanical point of failure. But other than that, it was largely trend-chasing. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [mlatu](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=mlatu) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26660148) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659880) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659987) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659988)\[–\]<br>\> Then haptic feedback made it so touching the screen feels better<br>ACTUAL<br>MECHANICAL<br>HAPTICS<br>vs<br>vibrating-slab-of-glass<br>vs<br>non-vibrating-slab-of-glass<br>_cough_ yeah, have fun with your glass bricks | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [GrinningFool](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=GrinningFool) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26661729) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659880) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26660148) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659988)\[–\]<br>Once 'haptic' found its way into phone marketing, 'a slab of glass that vibrates when you touch it' quickly supplanted any more complete definition of the word in this context. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [oblio](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=oblio) [on April 1, 2021](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659988) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26659880) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26659740#26660663)\[–\]<br>\> So who's the target for this 900 usd device?<br>The cynic in me says: No one. Or as close to no one as possible: too few people. And too fickle people (enthusiasts are notorious for being fickle and changing their minds about tech every 5 minutes). We just won't notice anything as this product dies a silent death, a few months from now. | |
